  <sharedRules>
    <rule name="Barricade Fortification" id="3d9c-eca1-5612-ad2b" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>A Barricade (a Fortification with the Barricade Sub-type) is treated as Battlefield Debris under the core 2.0 Fortification rules: it has no Armour Values or Hull Points, cannot be targeted by attacks unless noted, and does not block friendly movement unless impassable. It is deployed during the Fortifications deployment step within your Deployment Zone (unless modified by Infiltrate), and every section in a Barricade unit must be deployed in base-to-base contact with at least one other section in the unit. Models within 1" of a Barricade gain a 4+ Cover Save against shooting attacks made across the barricade, and enemy units charging across a Barricade treat it as Difficult Terrain.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Slow but Sure" id="fd19-24cc-00f6-3ef0" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>The Kin are short and heavily equipped. Votann Infantry have a Movement Characteristic of 5 unless their entry says otherwise, but may Advance and still shoot Rapid Fire weapons and declare charges in the same turn†.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Stealth" id="c4d7-f993-1a1d-9a93" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>If the model has not moved this turn, it cannot be targeted by ranged attacks from more than 12" away.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Stubborn Kin" id="b9c6-1e4f-c03f-56fb" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>Votann Infantry units may re-roll failed Morale checks while they have at least half their starting models.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Void Armour" id="aae4-08fe-8918-77c0" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>Votann void armour seals against hostile environments. Units with void armour (see Wargear) ignore the Pinning special rule from Blast and Template weapons†.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Eye of the Ancestors" id="9a27-359a-8678-d056" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>All Votann units have the Eye of the Ancestors special rule. It is made up of the following rules. Judgement Tokens. • An enemy unit can hold a maximum of 2 Judgement Tokens at any time, whatever their source. • Tokens are permanent unless a rule says otherwise. They stay until the unit is removed from play. • Some rules give temporary tokens, which are lost at the end of the turn. Temporary tokens count toward the maximum of 2. If a rule would give a unit more tokens than the maximum allows, the extra tokens are not gained. Marking the Grudge. After both players have deployed their armies, the Votann player selects up to two enemy units to each gain 1 Judgement Token. Units on the battlefield and units in Reserves can both be selected. See Pre-Game Sequence for exact timing. Accumulating Grudges. At the end of each Player Turn, each enemy unit that met one or more of the following conditions during that turn gains 1 Judgement Token. A unit gains only 1 token per turn this way, however many conditions it met. • Slayer of Kin: The enemy unit destroyed a friendly Votann unit. • Bane of the Ancestors: The enemy unit inflicted one or more unsaved Wounds or Hull Point losses on your Warlord or on a friendly Votann Independent Character. • Defiler of Claims: The enemy unit ended the turn within 3" of an Objective Marker that you controlled with a Votann unit at the start of that turn. Settling the Debt. When a Votann unit makes attacks against a marked unit, it gains the following cumulative benefits: • 1 (Marked for Reckoning): The Votann unit gains Preferred Enemy against that unit: it may re-roll To Hit and To Wound rolls of 1. • 2 (Blood-Oath Vendetta): As above. In addition, the Votann unit gains Hatred against that unit in the Assault phase, and its Ranged attacks against that unit gain Rending (6+). Any weapon that already has Rending (X), Breaching (X) or Rupture (X) instead improves each of those values by 1 against that unit (for example, 6+ becomes 5+), to a best of 3+.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Ancestral Lore (X)" id="7bc4-43dd-5e6c-2ad5" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>A model with the Psyker unit sub-type and Ancestral Lore (X) automatically knows the Ancestral Wrath Psychic Weapon, plus X Psychic Powers selected from the table below when building the army.

• Manifesting Powers: Each Psychic Power is used in the phase specified in its entry. To manifest a Psychic Power, make a Psychic Check (roll 2D6 equal to or under the Psyker's Leadership characteristic). If the check is failed, the power is not manifested and the Psyker immediately suffers Perils of the Warp as detailed in the Age of Darkness rulebook.
• Psychic Weapons: Ancestral Wrath is a ranged weapon fired during the Shooting phase (or during a Shooting phase Reaction, such as Return Fire or Overwatch) using standard shooting rules; it does not require a Psychic Check to fire, though it benefits from the Psychic Focus special rule if the Psyker has not manifested a Psychic Power earlier in that turn.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="League (X)" id="8aaa-89ad-e8cb-8d09" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>When building an army, choose one League for each Detachment. Every unit in that Detachment gains the League (X) special rule, where X is the chosen League. Unique Characters (such as Ûthar the Destined) may join any League and gain that Detachment's League (X) rule without breaking Detachment restrictions.</description>
    </rule>
    <rule name="Invulnerable Save (X)" id="a75a-fe58-e375-cdb8" hidden="false" publicationId="e1a3-795b-be05-784b">
      <description>The model has an Invulnerable Save of X, which may be taken against any Wound instead of its armour save.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
